\section{Phase II Directional-Weight Ablation}
\label{sec:lambda_supp}

Proposition~\ref{prop:compat} gives a tapering condition sufficient for the
penalized and free-Jones estimators to share a first-order limit, and
\eqref{eq:s_lambda_fixed} does not satisfy it. This section measures the
discrepancy that condition was introduced to bound, by sweeping the weight
itself.

\emph{Protocol.} The weight is swept over
$\lambda_k\in\{0,0.1,1,10\}$, common to all $K$ paths, for the three
receiver modes at $-10$, $0$, $10$, $20$ and $30$~dB, with $480$ trials per
cell: $28\,800$ estimator runs in total, none failed. Within a cell every
$\lambda_k$ arm consumes the identical noisy observation and the identical
Phase~I output, asserted by stored hashes at run time, so an arm-to-arm
difference is caused by the weight alone. The noise convention is the fixed
full-EVS reference variance used for the receiver-mode study of the main
paper, and $\lambda_k=1$ is the reported setting of
\eqref{eq:s_rdir_fixed}. The $\lambda_k=0$ arm is the free-Jones control:
the directional term is removed exactly, leaving only the
$10^{-10}$ diagonal loading that conditions the Jones solve in every arm.
The remaining Phase~II constants are those of
Section~\ref{sec:impl_supp}, and $\lambda_k$ is held fixed rather than
annealed, so the sweep varies the reported design and nothing else.

\emph{Result.} Table~\ref{tab:s_lambda} reports the ablation. Across the
$15$ cells the largest movement of inlier-conditional position RMSE over the
whole weight range is $8.2\times10^{-6}$~mm, at most $2$ parts in $10^5$ of
the value itself, and the largest movement of inlier-conditional clock RMSE
is $1.1\times10^{-5}$~ps, at most $3$ parts in $10^6$. In all $45$ paired
comparisons against $\lambda_k=1$, zero catastrophic trials are rescued and
zero introduced, so every McNemar exact test returns $p=1$ and the outlier
set of a cell is a property of the cell rather than of the weight. Paired
bootstrap intervals on the position-RMSE difference (Table~II of the
archived paired records, $2\,000$ replicates) contain zero in every cell
except a few whose half-widths are below $10^{-10}$~m. No high-SNR error
floor is attributable to the weight: at $30$~dB the $\lambda_k=10$ and
$\lambda_k=0$ arms differ by $3.9\times10^{-11}$~m for full EVS,
$6.2\times10^{-12}$~m for dual polarization and $4.9\times10^{-12}$~m for
scalar, while the corresponding RMSEs are $4.4\times10^{-6}$,
$1.0\times10^{-5}$ and $9.8\times10^{-5}$~m.

\emph{Mechanism.} The last column of Table~\ref{tab:s_lambda} explains the
inertness. After the anchor refresh of Section~\ref{sec:impl_supp} the
Phase~II minimizer already lies along $\hat\be_k$ to working precision, so
$\bx^{\mathsf H}\bR_{\rm dir}\bx$ evaluates at most to $3.8\times10^{-4}$ on
any trial in the sweep, never above $9.5\times10^{-5}$ of the data term, and
its cell medians run from $10^{-11}$ downward. The penalty is therefore
close to non-binding at the solution rather than binding and small, which is
the stronger statement: it is not that the bias is beneath the noise, but
that the constraint the weight imposes is nearly inactive. Consistently,
$\lambda_k\in\{0,0.1\}$ attains a raw data objective no worse than
$\lambda_k=1$ on essentially every trial, whereas $\lambda_k=10$ degrades it
measurably in one cell (full EVS at $-10$~dB, non-degradation rate
$3.5\%$) at a median cost of $2.6\times10^{-11}$ in objective and no
measurable cost in position.

First, the compatibility gap of
Proposition~\ref{prop:compat} is empirically vacuous over the tested region:
the reported estimator and its free-Jones counterpart return the same
estimates to within $2$ parts in $10^5$, so the free-Jones bound is a
reference for a data model this estimator matches in practice. This remains
an empirical statement at the tested SNRs and sample size, not a proof of
asymptotic efficiency for fixed $\lambda_k$; the tapering condition of
Proposition~\ref{prop:compat} is sufficient, and the sweep shows only that it
is not necessary here.
Second, the sweep reassigns the scalar high-SNR floor discussed in the main
paper. Scalar conditional RMSE saturates near $0.098$~mm at $30$~dB against a
falling PEB, but it saturates identically at $\lambda_k=0$ (the arms agree to
$4.9\times10^{-12}$~m), so the floor cannot be an artifact of the penalty.
It follows instead from the receiver: at $d_{\rm evs}=1$ the block
$\bA_k=\bc_k\otimes\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}$ has rank one, being the
Kronecker product of a vector with a $1\times2$ row, so the retained data
constrain the two-dimensional Jones state of path $k$ through a single linear
functional at any weight. The dual-polarized and full modes, whose blocks
have rank two, show no such saturation and track the noise level over the
same range.

