\section{C3 Clock-Unit Control}
\label{sec:c3control_supp}

The control itself is Table~\ref{main-tab:c3_control} of the main paper:
Phase~II is isolated by giving all four routes the same $100$ MKSC-GI
initializers, so the rows differ only in how the clock coordinate is
carried. Three points about how it was run are recorded here.

The unit-invariance entry is not an inference from agreeing digits. The
three clock parameterizations of the profiled route -- seconds,
nanoseconds and the range coordinate $c_0\Delta t$ -- were run as separate
arms and their returned $(\hat\bp,\widehat{\Delta t},\hat\bx)$ arrays
compared by stored hash; the hashes are equal, which is the strongest form
Theorem~\ref{main-thm:clock}(ii) can take in floating point, because the
clock never enters the outer iterate at all. The explicit 4-D arms differ
in the third significant digit of position RMSE across the same three
labels, which is what an unscaled coordinate does to a quasi-Newton
trajectory.

The evaluation counts are medians over the $100$ trials of the outer
L-BFGS-B iterate count, not of inner clock solves; each outer evaluation of
the profiled route additionally performs one certified clock solve over a
median of $2\,036$ grid points, whose cost is the $0.25$~s reported in
Section~\ref{main-subsec:res_cost}. Finally, the two mis-scaled labels lose
one trial in $100$ that the range-scaled and profiled routes keep; with a
single discordant pair this is an illustration of the scaling hazard, not a
resolved rate difference.

\section{Phase I Hyperparameter Sensitivity}
\label{sec:hypersens_supp}

The estimator carries several fixed Phase I constants that are design
choices rather than derived quantities. Each is swept here over the frozen
route at $-10$~dB, the operating point at which basin failures occur at all,
with $480$ trials per value and the same seed sequence across values, so
every comparison is paired and any difference is attributable to the swept
constant alone. Catastrophic events use the same $0.1$~m threshold as the
main text, and medians are inlier conditional.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Paired Phase I sensitivity at $-10$~dB, $480$ trials per value.
Boldface marks the frozen setting. ``Identical'' counts trials whose
position estimate is unchanged from the frozen setting to working
precision.}
\label{tab:hypersens}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.2pt}
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
Constant & Value & $P_{\rm cat}$ [\%] & Median [mm] & Identical\\
\midrule
Exact permutations   & \textbf{2} & $0.83$ & $0.309$ & ---\\
                     & $3$        & $0.83$ & $0.309$ & $480/480$\\
                     & $6=K!$     & $0.83$ & $0.309$ & $480/480$\\
\midrule
$w_{\rm clk}$        & $0$        & $0.00$ & $0.308$ & $475/480$\\
                     & $0.25$     & $0.62$ & $0.310$ & $479/480$\\
                     & \textbf{0.5} & $0.83$ & $0.309$ & ---\\
                     & $1.0$      & $0.83$ & $0.309$ & $480/480$\\
                     & $2.0$      & $0.83$ & $0.309$ & $480/480$\\
\midrule
Confidence weights   & \textbf{on} & $0.83$ & $0.309$ & ---\\
                     & off        & $0.83$ & $0.309$ & $1/480$\\
\midrule
Beam-space ranges    & $5$        & $0.83$ & $0.309$ & $480/480$\\
                     & \textbf{9} & $0.83$ & $0.309$ & ---\\
                     & $17$       & $0.83$ & $0.309$ & $480/480$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Three of the four constants are inert. Raising the association shortlist
from two to the exhaustive $K!=6$ reproduces the frozen estimate on every
one of the $480$ trials, so on this suite the two-tier search never
discarded the permutation that exact scoring would have selected; the
shortlist is a heuristic, but it did not bind here. The beam-space range
grid is likewise inert between $5$ and $17$ trial ranges, consistent with
the design, since trial ranges only seed an exact spherical scan and it is
direction, not range, that the aperture resolves. Disabling the
common-geometry confidence weights changes the numerical path on $479$ of
$480$ trials but moves no trial across the catastrophic threshold and leaves
the median unchanged, so the weights buy conditioning rather than outcomes.

The association clock weight behaves differently, and the result does not
favor the frozen choice. Values at or above $w_{\rm clk}=0.5$ are mutually
bit identical, but reducing the weight removes catastrophic trials rather
than adding them: $w_{\rm clk}=0.25$ rescues one and $w_{\rm clk}=0$, which
deletes the clock-consistency term from \eqref{main-eq:assoc_perm}
altogether, rescues all four, with none introduced in either direction. With
only four discordant pairs the effect is not resolved at this sample size,
McNemar's exact test giving $p=0.125$ against $w_{\rm clk}=0$ and $p=0.25$
against $w_{\rm clk}=0.25$, so we do not claim that deleting the clock term
improves the estimator. What we do report is that this suite provides no
evidence that the clock term improves basin capture at $-10$~dB. Its role in
\eqref{main-eq:assoc_perm} is structural, in that it couples the whole
permutation instead of scoring pairs independently, and that structural
statement is unaffected; the empirical benefit is not established here, and
a larger paired suite would be needed to settle it.

