\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_internal_snr.pdf}
\caption{Internal structure validation over the full campaign range,
$480$ paired trials per SNR point. The panels show position accuracy,
catastrophic-event rate, clock accuracy, and Jones-domain
channel-reconstruction NMSE; the two leftmost points lie below the
reported operating region.}
\label{fig:s_internal}
\end{figure*}

\section{Matched Compression Evidence Below the Region}
\label{sec:compression_below}

The variance-reduction effect of the Maxwell--Kronecker projection is
largest exactly where the estimator is most fragile
(Fig.~\ref{fig:s_compression}), and the single-factor protocol of the main
paper is unchanged: both arms are compressed everywhere except in the delay
subspace estimate. At $-15$~dB the p95 Phase~I delay error improves from
$28.8$ to $2.53$~ns, the basin acquisition rate from $93.33\%$ to
$98.33\%$, and the catastrophic rate from $6.04\%$ to $1.46\%$; at
$-20$~dB the p95 improves from $46.9$ to $28.4$~ns, the basin rate from
$81.04\%$ to $93.54\%$, and the catastrophic rate from $17.08\%$ to
$6.25\%$. Read against the operating-region factor of $35$ in the p95 at
$-10$~dB, compression shifts the onset of severe delay-pole collapse by
approximately $5$~dB, but it does not remove it: the compressed gap itself
reaches $0.81$ at $-20$~dB, against $0.99$ for the uncompressed one, and
both variants fail structurally once it approaches unity.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_compression.pdf}
\caption{Single-factor evaluation of the Maxwell--Kronecker projection over
the full campaign range. Panels as in
the main paper's single-factor compression test. In~(c) the
retained energy fraction approaches the white-noise floor $r/I=1/16$ at
$-20$~dB while the \emph{uncompressed} subspace-leakage ratio
$\sigma_{K+1}/\sigma_K$ reaches $0.99$ and the compressed one $0.81$;
smaller is better, so this is the diagnostic form of delay-pole collapse,
and the separation between the two curves is the SNR margin the
projection provides.}
\label{fig:s_compression}
\end{figure*}

\section{Receiver Modes Below the Region}

The mode ordering of the main paper is unchanged and its consequences are
amplified (Fig.~\ref{fig:s_receiver}). At $-15$~dB the catastrophic rates
are $45.83\%$ for the scalar mode, $12.29\%$ for the dual-polarized receiver,
and $2.08\%$ for
the full six-component receiver; at $-20$~dB they are $84.79\%$, $35.83\%$
and $5.00\%$. The matched free-Jones PEBs at $-20$~dB are $7.76$, $3.14$
and $1.35$~mm, so the bound ratio between the scalar and the full receiver
is $5.7\times$, identical to its value at $-10$~dB, because the noise
variance enters every mode's EFIM as a common factor and the mode weights
themselves are noise independent,
while the failure-rate ratio is $17\times$. The gap between the two ratios
is the same one identified in the main paper, and it grows rather than
closes as the SNR falls: component reduction decreases receive energy and
can also eliminate panel separability, and that latter loss is structural,
not recovered by increasing SNR. The
efficiency ratios of the masked modes here are conditioned on a heavily
truncated inlier subset (at $-20$~dB the scalar mode retains one trial
in seven) and are reported for completeness only.

