\caption{Receiver-mode study over the full campaign range under nested
component masks at a common absolute noise level. Panels extend
Fig.~\ref{main-fig:c1_receiver} of the main paper. The efficiency ratios
of the masked modes below $-10$~dB
are conditioned on a small inlier subset and are reported for completeness
only.}
\label{fig:s_receiver}
\end{figure*}

\section{External Benchmark and Synchronization Below the Region}

Below the operating region, among the currently evaluated native-readout
adaptations the proposed route's margin widens rather than reversing
(Fig.~\ref{fig:s_benchmark} and Table~\ref{tab:s_benchmark}): it holds the
lowest median, p95 and catastrophic rate at both points. The evaluated
near-field OMP adaptation -- whose exhaustive dictionary search needs no
initializer -- no longer provides a lower-catastrophic-rate fallback: its
far-field direction-acquisition stage fails to bracket a reliable basin on
essentially every trial at $-15$ and $-20$~dB (catastrophic rate $100\%$
at both, against $1.25\%$ and $5.00\%$ for the proposed route). The
crossover argument of earlier campaigns -- that an initializer-free grid
search trades resolution for low-SNR robustness -- therefore does not
hold for this evaluated adaptation once its acquisition stage is
far-field while the scene is deep near-field with a blocked direct path;
this is a property of
the evaluated adaptation on this scene, not a general claim about
uniform dominance outside the tested region or about the reference
method's capability. The two in-model routes degrade as in the operating
region, only more so: range-scaled 4-D VP loses its basin four to five
times as often as the proposed route ($22.08\%$ against $5.00\%$ at
$-20$~dB, $6.88\%$ against $1.25\%$ at $-15$~dB), and the Algebraic CPD
adaptation, already carrying a $34$--$37\%$ high-SNR read-out floor,
degrades to $56$--$72\%$.

The clock comparison extends into the threshold region on the same
realizations (Fig.~\ref{fig:s_benchmark_clock}), and the synchronization
ranking tracks the position ranking here as well, without a crossover. The
proposed route keeps a clock-catastrophic rate of $1.04\%$ at $-15$~dB and
$3.65\%$ at $-20$~dB, against $5.42\%$ and $17.81\%$ for the range-scaled
4-D route; near-field OMP instead carries a $3.4$--$3.5$-ns median with
clock-catastrophic rates of $89.17\%$ and $87.81\%$, VBI/SBL a $14.58\%$
and $24.58\%$ outlier mass consistent with its position floor, and
Algebraic CPD the largest ($12.71\%$ and $31.35\%$).

