\section*{Part III: Auxiliary Derivations and Extended Proofs}

\section{Local Identifiability with Unknown Jones Nuisance}
\label{sec:ident_proof_supp}

Let $(\bchi_\star,\bx_\star)$ be the true parameters, let
$\bD_\chi=[\partial(\bA\bx_\star)/\partial\chi_i]_{i=1}^4$, and define
\begin{equation}
 \widetilde{\bD}_\chi=
 \begin{bmatrix}
 \Real\{\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\}\\
 \Imag\{\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\}
 \end{bmatrix},\qquad
 \bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}=\bI-\bA_\star\bA_\star^\dagger.
 \label{eq:s_ident}
\end{equation}

\begin{proposition}[Local position--clock identifiability]
Suppose $\bA_\star$ has constant rank near the truth, the paths are
resolvable in the raw OFDM domain, the panel assignment is fixed, the truth
is interior, and $\rank_{\R}(\widetilde{\bD}_\chi)=4$. Then $\bchi_\star$
is first-order locally identifiable after eliminating the complex Jones
coefficients. Under white Gaussian noise this rank condition is equivalent
to positive definiteness of the free-Jones EFIM.
\end{proposition}

\begin{IEEEproof}
A first-order perturbation is
$\delta\bmu=\bD_\chi\bq+\bA_\star\delta\bx$ with $\bq\in\R^4$. A nonzero
geometric direction can be canceled by a Jones perturbation exactly when
$\bD_\chi\bq\in\coll(\bA_\star)$, or equivalently
$\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\bq=\mathbf0$. Realifying this map gives
\eqref{eq:s_ident}, whose nullspace is trivial precisely at real rank four.
Its Gram matrix is
$\Real\{\bD_\chi^{\mathsf H}\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\}$, proportional
to the free-Jones EFIM, proving the equivalence.
\end{IEEEproof}

The statement is local and conditional. In particular, three panels are
not sufficient by equation counting alone: the RIS codes must preserve the
near-field spatial tangents, the curvature must be nondegenerate, and the
panel geometry must avoid collinearity and symmetry. Global permutations
and finite-bandwidth ambiguities are not excluded.

\section{Nested-Component Information Order}
\label{sec:split_proof_supp}

Theorem~\ref{main-thm:split} and its proof, including the Kronecker
projector splitting lemma on which both parts rest, are in the main paper.
Two remarks extend them.

First, the exact single-panel ratio
$\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}=\|\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta\bx\|^2/\|\bTheta\bx\|^2$
depends on $k$ once several panels are resolved, so the weighted sum over
panels is in general not a global rescaling of the aggregate EFIM. The main
paper states the block-orthogonal approximation this admits; nothing beyond
the single-panel identity is claimed exactly.

Second, for \emph{nested} component sets the free-Jones information order
follows without any assumption on the Jones block, from the variational
identity
\begin{equation}
 \bu^{\mathsf T}\bJ_\chi^{\rm free}\bu
 =\frac{2}{\sigma^2}\min_{\delta\bx}
 \|\bD_\chi\bu+\bA\delta\bx\|^2 .
\end{equation}
Observing additional components appends nonnegative residual terms to the
minimized quadratic form, which therefore cannot decrease. This is why the
scalar $\preceq$ dual $\preceq$ full ordering of the reported PEBs holds
even where a mask makes the retained Jones block singular and the exact
ratio $\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}$ is not by itself enough to order the
aggregate.

\section{Scope of the Three Projection Roles}
\label{sec:c2scope_supp}

The Maxwell--Kronecker projection plays three roles that the main paper
distinguishes but does not separate at length. Sufficiency and EFIM
preservation apply to the matched statistical model and require the
white-noise model of the main paper. The coupled recovery is equivariant only
\emph{after} the observation is projected, so it is the raw recovery applied
to a projected observation and can change the estimate through noise
suppression. Only the likelihood isometry is deterministic, holding pointwise
for every candidate and any observation, so it can move neither the final
objective nor its optimum. Pole preservation adds four conditions, namely
distinct poles, adequate smoothing dimensions, nonzero paths, and full
compressed snapshot rank, which the implementation records instead of
assuming. Under colored noise an optimal processor would whiten or use a
covariance-weighted projector, so the unwhitened sweep of the main paper is a
mismatch test and not an extension of Theorem~\ref{main-thm:compress}.

\section{Compression: Reach of the Argument and Its Degeneracies}
\label{sec:compress_proof_supp}

Theorem~\ref{main-thm:compress} is proved in the main paper. Two points
about its reach are recorded here.

The EFIM step uses only semi-unitarity of $\bU_S$, so the same argument
proves preservation under \emph{any} semi-unitary map whose range contains
both $\coll(\bA)$ and $\coll(\bD_\chi)$; the specific aggregate
Maxwell--Kronecker basis is one convenient such map, not the only one, and
it is the one that can be built without any UE-dependent quantity.

The generic-rank claim of part~(iv) is a measure-zero statement and
excludes named structural degeneracies rather than all of them. Each
$K\times K$ minor of the compressed non-frequency snapshot matrix is real
analytic in the continuously distributed training phases, Jones states and
nonzero gains, so a single feasible design with a nonzero minor implies
that this minor is not identically zero and its zero set is null. What this
does not cover, and what the implementation therefore records per trial
instead of assuming, is $rT<K$, an annihilated path, a zero gain, or
systematically dependent panel signatures.

\section{Coupled-Recovery Lift and Exact Scale Elimination}
\label{sec:technical_proofs_supp}

\begin{lemma}[Equivariant coupled-recovery lift]
Let $\mathcal F$ map a Hankel tensor to its recovered EVS and RIS factors
through main-paper Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:coupledls_short} and a leading rank-one
split. Then
\begin{equation}
 \bQ_S\mathcal F(\mathcal Z\times_1\bQ_S^{\mathsf H})
 =\mathcal F(\mathcal Z\times_1\bQ_S\bQ_S^{\mathsf H}),
 \label{eq:s_lift}
\end{equation}
up to the singular-vector phase convention.
\end{lemma}

\begin{IEEEproof}
The coupled least-squares map acts on the EVS mode by left multiplication,
which commutes with $\bQ_S^{\mathsf H}$. In addition,
$(\bQ_S\bM)^{\mathsf H}(\bQ_S\bM)=\bM^{\mathsf H}\bM$, so $\bQ_S\bM$
and $\bM$ share singular values and right singular vectors, with left
singular vectors related by multiplication by $\bQ_S$.
\end{IEEEproof}

Thus compressed recovery is the raw recovery on a projected observation,
not an identical estimator on the original noisy data. It removes the
$I-r$ signal-free EVS directions and can improve variance; that empirical
effect is kept distinct from the exact Phase~II isometry.

\begin{proposition}[Exact complex-scale elimination]
For every $\bp$ with $\bh_k(\bp)\ne\mathbf0$,
\begin{equation}
 \min_{\alpha_k\in\C}\|\tilde\bt_k-\alpha_k\bh_k(\bp)\|^2
 =\|\bPi_{\bh_k(\bp)}^\perp\tilde\bt_k\|^2,
 \quad
 \hat\alpha_k=\frac{\bh_k^{\mathsf H}\tilde\bt_k}{\|\bh_k\|^2}.
 \label{eq:s_scale}
\end{equation}
The profiled term is invariant to every nonzero complex rescaling of
$\bh_k$ and every phase rotation of $\tilde\bt_k$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{IEEEproof}
For fixed $\bp$, the objective is a strictly convex quadratic in the real
and imaginary parts of $\alpha_k$. Setting the Wirtinger derivative with
respect to $\alpha_k^\ast$ to zero yields \eqref{eq:s_scale}; substitution
gives the orthogonal residual. An orthogonal projector depends only on the
one-dimensional span of $\bh_k$, and its output norm is unchanged by a
phase rotation of the input.
\end{IEEEproof}

\section{Clock Profile: Solver Invariants and Screening Bounds}
\label{sec:clock_proof_supp}

Theorem~\ref{main-thm:clock} is proved in the main paper. The solver-side
details it abstracts are the following.

Alongside the curvature bound $L_2$ used in main-paper
Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:bound_short}, termwise differentiation of main-paper
Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:trig_short} also gives the first-derivative bound
\begin{equation}
 |Q'(t)|\le(2\pi\Delta f)\sum_{\ell}|\ell|\,|c_\ell| ,
\end{equation}
uniform on $\mathcal T$. This is what makes the incumbent screening cheap:
an interval can be discarded on the Lipschitz test before its quadratic
upper bound is formed.

The branch-and-bound loop maintains three invariants: a finite cover of
$\mathcal T$; an upper bound for every surviving interval; and an incumbent
supplied by FFT screening and Newton refinement. Only intervals whose upper
bound falls below the incumbent are discarded, so the cover always contains
a global maximizer and the largest surviving bound $U$ dominates it. The
certificate is therefore an a~posteriori quantity valid under any stopping
rule, which is why reaching the subdivision cap
$N_{\rm cap}=2\times10^{4}$ would invalidate the tolerance test but not the
reported gap. The correctness of the bound and the empirical success of the
test are two distinct facts, and
Section~\ref{sec:cert_supp} reports the second separately.

\section{Directional Regularization: Conditional Compatibility}
\label{sec:regularization_proof_supp}

Write $\bvt=[\bchi^{\mathsf T},\Real\{\bx\}^{\mathsf T},
\Imag\{\bx\}^{\mathsf T}]^{\mathsf T}$ for the realified full parameter
vector, so that $\hat\bvt_{\rm free}$ and $\hat\bvt_{\rm reg}$ denote the
unpenalized and directionally penalized estimates of Phase~II; every
gradient and Hessian below is taken with respect to this real
parameterization.

\begin{proposition}[Conditional asymptotic compatibility]
\label{prop:compat}
For a fixed design with $\sigma\to0$, suppose the truth $\bvt_\star$ is
interior, the free-Jones Fisher information is nonsingular, and the free
estimator reaches the correct local basin with probability tending to one.
If the penalty
$\mathcal R_\sigma$ satisfies, for every fixed $C<\infty$,
\begin{equation}
 \|\nabla\mathcal R_\sigma(\bvt_\star)\|=o_{\mathsf P}(\sigma),\qquad
 \sup_{\|\bvt-\bvt_\star\|\le C\sigma}
 \|\nabla^2\mathcal R_\sigma(\bvt)\|=o_{\mathsf P}(1),
 \label{eq:s_taper}
\end{equation}
then the regularized and free estimates in that basin differ by
$o_{\mathsf P}(\sigma)$ and have the same first-order limit distribution.
For the Gram-scaled directional penalty, anchor consistency
$O_{\mathsf P}(\sigma)$ together with
$\max_k\lambda_{k,\sigma}=o_{\mathsf P}(1)$ is sufficient.
\end{proposition}

\begin{IEEEproof}
Write the penalized objective as
$\mathcal L_\sigma=\mathcal L+\mathcal R_\sigma$. The free score at the
truth is $O_{\mathsf P}(\sigma)$ and the free Hessian converges uniformly on
an $O(\sigma)$ ball to a nonsingular matrix $\bJ_0$. Expanding the two
stationarity conditions about the truth and subtracting gives
\begin{equation}
 [\bJ_0+o_{\mathsf P}(1)](\hat\bvt_{\rm reg}-\hat\bvt_{\rm free})
 =-\nabla\mathcal R_\sigma(\bvt_\star)
 -\bE_\sigma(\hat\bvt_{\rm reg}-\bvt_\star),
\end{equation}
where $\|\bE_\sigma\|=o_{\mathsf P}(1)$ by \eqref{eq:s_taper}. Both
right-hand terms are $o_{\mathsf P}(\sigma)$, and inversion proves the
claim. For the directional penalty, its gradient at the truth is
proportional to the product of the weight and anchor-direction error, while
its Hessian is of the order of the weight relative to the data Gram, giving
the stated sufficient conditions.
\end{IEEEproof}

In every reported experiment the directional weight is fixed at
\begin{equation}
 \lambda_k=1,\qquad k=1,\ldots,K,
 \label{eq:s_lambda_fixed}
\end{equation}
so that main-paper Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:Rdir_short} reduces to
\begin{equation}
 \bR_{\rm dir}(\bp)=\blkdiag_{k=1}^{K}
 \left[\tfrac12\tr\bG_{0,kk}(\bp)\,\bPi_{\hat\be_k}^{\perp}\right].
 \label{eq:s_rdir_fixed}
\end{equation}
The penalty is scaled by the Jones data Gram but is neither path adaptive
nor annealed with SNR. Its Jones-direction curvature therefore remains of
the same order as that of the corresponding data term at every noise level.
In particular $\max_k\lambda_{k,\sigma}=o_{\mathsf P}(1)$ does not hold for
the reported estimator. Proposition~\ref{prop:compat} thus characterizes
the tapering condition sufficient for asymptotic compatibility with the
free-Jones model, rather than an asymptotic property of the fixed-weight
estimator evaluated in this paper. Consequently nothing in that proposition
establishes that the reported estimator attains the free-Jones bound. The
bound is used only as a reference for the unpenalized data model, and the
observed RMSE/PEB proximity is reported as finite-SNR empirical behavior.
That condition is sufficient and not necessary, however, and
Section~\ref{sec:lambda_supp} measures the quantity it was introduced to
bound directly: over $\lambda_k\in\{0,0.1,1,10\}$ and $28\,800$ paired runs
the penalized and free-Jones estimates agree to within $2$ parts in $10^5$
in every cell, because the penalty is very nearly inactive at the
solution. The failure of the tapering condition is therefore without
measurable consequence over the tested region, which is an empirical
statement at those SNRs and sample sizes and still not a proof of asymptotic
efficiency.

